## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a safe, efficient memory primitive from scratch, focusing on low-level concurrency and memory layout rather than library calls.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size allocators and arena preallocation
- Free-list embedded in blocks
- Lock-free allocation/free with atomic CAS
- Memory alignment and fragmentation basics
- Basic multi-threaded testing strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch in C-like pseudocode
#include <stdatomic.h>
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char data[64]; } Block;
static unsigned char arena[64*1024];
_Atomic(Block*) head;
void init_pool() { head = NULL; /* link blocks in arena */ }
void* alloc_block() { Block* b; while ((b = atomic_load(&head)) && !atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, (Block**)&head, (Block*)b->next)); return b ? b->data : NULL; }
void free_block(void* p) { Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, data)); b->next = atomic_load(&head); while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, (Block**)&head, b)); }
```

